\section{Question 1 (8 $\times$ 8 Points $=$ 64 Points $+$ 4 EC Points)}

Assume we are interested in properties of the following
statistical process: First, a $n$--sided die ($n \geq 2$) is rolled once
(see Figure~\ref{Dice} for examples of such dice).
Suppose the outcome
is $k$, where $k \in \{ 1, \ldots n \}$. Now, this same $n$--sided die
is rolled $k$ times. We are interested in the distributional
properties of a random variable called $D$ that represents
the {\bf sum} of these $k$ dice rolls (but ignoring the outcome
of the very first die roll in the calculation of the sum).

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=3in]{HW02_dice.jpg}
\caption{\label{Dice}
Examples of 4--, 6--, 8--, 10--, 12--, and 20--sided dice,
obtained from \url{http://jackadreams.info/2012/11/25/dice-divination/}.}
\end{center}
\end{figure}


\begin{enumerate}

\item Write an R function that simulates a $n$--sided die ($n \geq 2$)
and then determines the sum of $k$ dice rolls of the same $n$--sided die.
The return value of this function should be just this sum.

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item Write a function that simulates $m$ of these two--staged dice rolls.
For the following parts, work with $m = 10,000$.
However, to speed up
the calculations and the translation of your R/Sweave/\LaTeX\ report, 
reduce $m$ initially.

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item Provide at least two different graphical summaries of your experiment for
$n = 4$ and $n = 6$ that show the sampling distribution of the 
sum of the dice rolls. Keep in mind that these are discrete distributions ---
so do not over--simplify your plots. Fine--tune your figures for the 
resulting pdf version and not for the screen version. 

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item What are $E(D)$ and $Var(D)$, based on your $m$ simulation runs
for $n = 4$ and $n = 6$?
And what is a 90\% confidence interval for the mean of the sums?

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item {\it Manually} tabulate all possible ordered sequences in a \LaTeX\ table
when $n = 2$ and $n = 3$. There should be 6 and 39 such sequences,
respectively. For each sequence, add two additional columns
that list the sum $d$ and the probability $p$ for each of these
sequences. Indicate the probabilities as a fraction and not as
a decimal. Then, create two more tables that only list the
sums $d$ and the probalities for these sums. Check in each
table that the probabilities sum up to exactly 1.


\item What is the {\bf exact} result for $E(D)$ and $Var(D)$ 
for $n = 4$ and $n = 6$?

To obtain these results, generalize your hand calculations from the
previous part and write an R function to obtain these tables and
probabilities. Include these computer--generated results here,
but only for $n = 2$ and $n = 3$.
Compare these results with those from
your manual calculations and make sure they are matching.
If not, check whether you made a mistake in your manual
calculations or in your R code.

Then, use simple summation (as introduced in any intro stats course)
to determine $E(D)$ and $Var(D)$ for $n = 4$ and $n = 6$. Be careful 
that you work with the proper probabilities. 

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@


\item Determine $P(D < 5)$ and $P(12 \leq D \leq 16)$
for $n = 4$ and $n = 6$, based on your simulation results.
Also use the results from the previous part 
to obtain these exact probabilities.
Be careful with the end points!
Create a table in \LaTeX\
that lists the simulation and exact results for these
probabilities, the absolute differences ($\mid$ simulated $-$ exact $\mid$),
and the relative differences (simulated $/$ exact $\cdot 100\%$).
Round to two decimal digits.

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@


\item Summarize your results. Was $m = 10,000$ a good choice,
or would it be (highly) beneficial to increase $m$?


\item (4 EC Points) 
Find a solution based on Mathematical Statistics 
for $E(D)$ and $Var(D)$ for $n = 4$ and $n = 6$ and write it up in \LaTeX. 
If you are interested in my \LaTeX\ lecture notes for Stat 6710/6720 as 
a template, I can send those upon request.


\end{enumerate}


