\section{Question 2 (5 $\times$ 8 Points $=$ 40 Points)}

Obtain a Monte Carlo estimate for the base of the natural logarithm, $e$.
This question is based on the concept we used to
find an estimate of $\pi$ via simulation.
We do {\bf not} want to calculate $e$ via any of the mathematical
formulas we know from Mathematical Statistics here.
Your results must be fully reproducible!

As a reminder, it holds that
\[
\int_1^e \frac{1}{x} dx = \ln(e) - \ln (1) = 1 - 0 = 1.
\]
Sketch a graph that shows this fact and use this as the basis
of your simulation. You can assume that $e \leq 4$.

{\bf Hint:} You need to find the smallest of the sorted $x$--values
such that the area underneath the curve $1/x$ inside the
rectangle $[1, x] \times [0, 1]$ is $\geq 1$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item Your estimate of $e$ should be based on 
$m = 1,000$, $m = 10,000$ and $m = 100,000$ pairs of random numbers.
First write a function that creates a single pair of these random numbers.
Then write a function that calls your first function $m$ times.
The return value of this function should be a single estimate of $e$.

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@


\item Create plots of the first four simulation runs for $m = 1,000$
that highlight the points that contribute to the calculation
of $e$, the area of interest, and the estimate of $e$ for that
specific simulation run. Optimize the spacing and size of these plots
and arrange them in a single figure. 

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item Obtain results for 100 and 1,000 replications for the 
three different values of $m$, i.e., you are conducting 
$2 \times 3 = 6$ different simulation experiments.
Tabulate the mean, median, and a 95\% confidence interval
for each of these six experiments. How close
do you get to the true $e$?

Also, create six boxplots in a $2 \times 3$ layout.
Label them clearly. Recall the principle for small multiples
that must be applied here.

{\bf Note:} 1,000 replications of $m = 100,000$ pairs will
take a while. Test your code (R \& \LaTeX) initially for smaller
values and only use the larger numbers when everything seems
to be correct.

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item Tabulate results from the expression
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{1}{n} \right)^n,
\]
for $n = 100$, 1,000, 10,000, and 100,000
and compare with your simulation results. 
Which seems to be the better way to obtain $e$ --- and why?

<<>>=
### Your R code here
@

\item This is a tricky question that requires some careful mathematical
and geometrical
considerations and not only some fast implementation of R code.
In this last part, you have to provide a verbal explanation of your
algorithm --- and why it is expected to give us a correct result.

{\bf Note:} As a reminder, make sure that your figures and tables are properly numbered
and have a meaningful caption --- and that you refer to each of them
at least once from the main text.

\end{enumerate}


